    "body": "Migrates `src/language` to Jane Street Core. **Zero downstream edits** were needed.\n\nTwo commits, reviewable separately:\n\n1. **`Make equality explicit at the type in src/language`** \u2014 126 sites, no Core involved. Names the equality witness at each comparison (`String.equal`, `Id.equal`, `Bigint.equal`, or a `[@deriving eq]`-generated one). This lands standalone and is worth reading on its own.\n2. **`Migrate src/language to Jane Street Core`** \u2014 the flip.\n\n**Equality strategy.** `int` needs nothing (Core permits `=` there, ~92 sites). Concrete types name their witness. Whole-term structural comparison uses `Poly` \u2014 26 explicit `Poly.equal` plus `open Poly` in the four modules whose *job* is structural comparison (`Equality`, `MatchExp`, `DrvTermBase`, `Operators`). `Typ.re` deliberately stays under Core's discipline with its sites fixed individually; it's where an accidental equality matters most.\n\n**Traps worth knowing about for review.** Several stdlib functions have same-named *total* counterparts in Core, so the flip changes the return type rather than failing to compile \u2014 `List.hd`/`tl`/`nth`/`find` become options and only break at the use site. `IdTagged.rep_id = List.hd(ids)` silently became `Id.t option` and surfaced two modules away. All swept to `_exn`. `Float.modf` returns a record, not a tuple, and `BuiltinsBase.re` uses `Stdlib.ceil` etc. deliberately \u2014 those back Hazel's *language-level* float builtins, so they need to be exact rather than a plausible Core equivalent.\n\n## Verification\n`make dev` clean \u00b7 `make test-quick` green (3448) \u00b7 `dune build --profile release src/language` warning-free.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
